From spencer.graves at PDF.COM  Mon Sep  1 09:45:18 2003
From: spencer.graves at PDF.COM (Spencer Graves)
Date: Mon, 01 Sep 2003 02:45:18 -0700
Subject: Re: Question re DF for testing covariate in lme() (W2k)
References: <OFE85D162C.80987354-ON80256D94.002E7C90@ema.lilly.com>
Message-ID: <3F53152E.1040504@pdf.com>

	  Have you looked at Pinhiero and Bates (2000) Mixed-Effects Models in 
S and S-Plus (Springer)?  That book is, I think, the definitive 
description of "lme".

	  You get 1 denominator degree of freedom for testing StorageDays, 
because you effectively only have 3 observations to evaluate the linear 
regression on StorageDays:  StorageDays 0 was measured only with 
AssayOcc = 1;  StorageDays 4 was measured only with AssayOcc = 2, and 
StorageDays  7 was measured only with AssayOcc = 3.  You could have 
measured each AssayOcc a million times, and it would not change the fact 
that you only have 3 AssayOcc's, and the effect of StorageDays appears 
only between AssayOcc, not within.

	  The comparison of different strengths occurs within AssayOcc, and 
therefore gets different denominators and different degrees of freedom 
than the evaluation of StorageDays.

hope this helps.  spencer graves

