"Smith, Stephen" <SmithSJ at mar.dfo-mpo.gc.ca> writes:

> I am trying to fit a nlme model for growth curves with weights=varfixed and
> the grouping variable being set to locations that the animals were sampled
> at.? I get the following error:
> 
> > nlme.formula(model = HEIGHT ~ von.b.derive.k(AGE, Linf, k, t0), data =
> June02hgtwgt, fixed = Linf + k +
> + t0 ~ 1, random = Linf + k + t0 ~ 1 | as.factor(TOW.NO), start = c(Linf =
> 126, k = -0.7, t0 =
> 0.1),subset=reg.weights>0,weights=varFixed(~reg.weights),na.action=na.omit)
> 
> Problem in conLin$Xy * varWeights(object): length of longer operand (542)
> should be a multiple of length of shorter (322), wh
> 
> ile calling subroutine setup_min
> Use traceback() to see the call stack
> 
> There are 542 non-zero entries in reg.weights (and a total of 592 records with
> no missing values for AGE and HEIGHT measured at 14 locations).
> 
> Using the debugger (some output given below) I found that the dimensions of
> conLin$Xy are 46 rows by 7 columns and length(varWeights(object)) = 542.?
> conLin is identified as the condensed linear object.? Can someone explain to
> me what the columns and rows of conLin$Xy refer to??

conLin is an internal representation of a linear mixed-effects model
used in the lme step.  (The nlme function alternates between lme steps
and penalized nonlinear least squares steps when performing parameter
estimation.) The columns of conLin$Xy are the derivatives of the model
function with respect to the random effects and the fixed effects
followed by the working residuals.  In your case the fourth through
the sixth columns should be repetitions of the first through the
third.

There is a point in the lme optimization where the information in Xy
is condensed by means of a series of QR decompositions.  There will be
3 rows (the dimension of the random effects vector) retained for each
group, 3 rows for the fixed effects and 1 row for the response.
That's where the 46 rows come from.

