From spencer.graves at PDF.COM  Thu Sep  4 11:33:11 2003
From: spencer.graves at PDF.COM (Spencer Graves)
Date: Thu, 04 Sep 2003 04:33:11 -0700
Subject: Re: lme() - Confidence interval for fitted values ?
References: <OFE456729E.FBC170ED-ON80256D97.00347A61@ema.lilly.com>
Message-ID: <3F5722F7.6040108@pdf.com>

"predict.lm" contains arguments se.fit and ci.fit, but these are not

available in the released versions of lme provided with S-Plus 6.1.  The

available theory should appear in any good book that treats least 
squares at an appropriate level (such as Draper and Smith?):  Suppose we 
fit:

	y = X b + e, e~N(0,Sig)

Suppose further we want to predict for X2.  Then 	

	var(y.hat) = X2 %*% inverse(t(X)%*%inverse(Sig)%*%X) %*% t(X2)

	  The hard part for lme is deciding what goes in X and what is Sig:  If 
you want a confidence interval on y for a particular subject included in 
X, then that subject is part of X;  otherwise, it must be included in Sig.

	  Summaries produced by the lme package provide the numbers required to 
compute this.  Have you looked at Pinhiero and Bates (2000) 
Mixed-Effects Models in S and S-Plus (Springer)?

	  Does this help address your question?

spencer graves

