From gary at sabot.com  Thu Sep 11 14:17:26 2003
From: gary at sabot.com (Gary Sabot)
Date: Thu, 11 Sep 2003 10:17:26 -0400
Subject: Re: vector conditionals
In-Reply-To: <5.1.0.14.2.20030911094841.01ffda88@127.0.0.1>
References: <20030911131642.8601.qmail@brain.delphioutpost.com> <5.1.0.14.2.20030911094841.01ffda88@127.0.0.1>
Message-ID: <16224.33782.912340.390248@brain.delphioutpost.com>

   To: Gary Sabot <gary at sabot.com>
   From: John Fox <jfox at mcmaster.ca>
   Date: Thu, 11 Sep 2003 09:52:12 -0400
   Subject: Re: [S] vector conditionals
   
   Dear Gary,
   
   ifelse *is* a vectorized conditional, and (unless I misunderstand what you 
   want to do more generally) your example can be reproduced using cascading 
   ifelses:
   
    > ifelse(c(a=F,b=T,c=F,d=F,e=F,f=T), 1:6,
   +     ifelse(c(T,F,F,F,F,F),7:12,
   +         ifelse(c(F,F,T,F,T,T),12, "default")))
       a   b    c         d    e   f
     "7" "2" "12" "default" "12" "6"
   
   I hope that this helps,
     John
   
Thanks, that does help.  I was trying to avoid doing ifelse() directly
because the nested syntax was obscuring what was going on (I think I
had 6 different cases that I was branching on, it really was most
appropriate for a vector version of switch()).  But I see now that I
can greatly clean up my implementation by making it use recursive
ifelse()'s to do the real work.  

The syntax is I guess a matter of taste.  But especially when the cond
and exprs are non-trivial, I find this kind of thing:

if.else.n(cond1,expr1,
          cond2,expr2,
          cond3,expr3,
          cond4,expr4)

to be easier to read and modify than this:

ifelse(cond1,expr1,
   ifelse(cond2,expr2,
     ifelse(cond3,expr3,
       ifelse(cond4,expr4,NA))))

As far as the semantics, a cascaded ifelse is happy to use rep() to
extend any vectors that are too short, but I prefer to treat that
shape mismatch as an error.  I am also treating an NA in a condition
as F, rather than passing it through to the output.

--gary

P.S. Here is the simplified code:

ifelse.n <- function(bool1,val1,...) 
{
   default <- NA
   arg.count <- nDotArgs(...)
   if((arg.count%%2)!=0) {
      #odd number of args, error
      stop("odd number of arguments to ifelse.vec")
   }
   dot.pair.count <- arg.count/2
   l <- length(bool1)
   result <- ifelse.n.internal(l,default,dot.pair.count,bool1,val1,...)
   result
}

ifelse.n.internal <- function(l,default,dot.pair.count,bool1,val1,...) {
   if((length(bool1)>1) && l!=length(bool1)) {
      stop("length of a boolean vector value does not match first boolean")
   }
   if((length(val1)>1) && l!=length(val1)) {
      stop("length of a value vector does not match first boolean")
   }
   #treat NA bool as if it were F.  Otherwise, output gets NA in this position
   bool1[is.na(bool1)] <- F
   if(dot.pair.count>=1) {
      ifelse(bool1,val1,ifelse.n.internal(l,default,dot.pair.count-1,...))
   } else {
      ifelse(bool1,val1,default)
   }
}

