From bates at stat.wisc.edu  Fri Sep 12 12:14:17 2003
From: bates at stat.wisc.edu (Douglas Bates)
Date: Fri, 12 Sep 2003 07:14:17 -0500
Subject: Re: R^2 for nonlinear regression
In-Reply-To: <1063319449.3f60f79972d6e@webmail3.its.uiowa.edu>
References: <1063319449.3f60f79972d6e@webmail3.its.uiowa.edu>
Message-ID: <6r65jyw6ee.fsf@bates4.stat.wisc.edu>

nidal-al-huniti at uiowa.edu writes:

> I am interested to know why we can't use R^2 in model selection for
> nonlinear regression.

Because it is not always meaningful.  The R^2 value for a linear model
results from comparing the linear model fit to that of a model with
only a constant term.  For a nonlinear model we cannot easily
determine if the model with only a constant term is nested within the
given model.  If not, the R^2 calculation is meaningless.

-- 
