Dear Professor Ripley,

Amazing! I had imported the data though MS-Access then MS-Excel into S.
And I certainly did not even pay any attention to the ordering of
factors, and till now didn't even think about this issue! I assumed that
S had treated the 'factors' as numbers.

You were spot on. The outcome measure (P=PET, there was no small 'p')
was ordered automatically by S as "1", "0", instead of the other way
around. The third outcome d was ordered similarly in the wrong way, thus
gave expected readings. Then the remaining s and b had the odds ratio
(of success or P=1) that came out in the 'wrong direction'. 

You are truly a gangster! What was most impressive was the ability to
spot the error without direct access to the data and amidst a very
confusing message. My amateur efforts are painfully illustrated by the
blatant disregard for case sensitivity. The P is the outcome which is
also PET, with no small 'p' and the final P in the table, was referring
to the P values.

One thing I do notice, is that epidemiologists / medical statisticians
like to interpret / display the results of logistic regression very
differently to that of applied mathematicians.

I am deeply grateful for your help, especially over the weekend. I have
learnt the importance of checking the ordering of factors in an S data
file.

Gratefully,

Eric Lim

